  "query_preview": "..(Saturday through Thursday)\nIssued at 345 PM EDT THU OCT 1 2026\n\nThe extended period opens during what looks to be a cool and\nrainy weekend. A cold front is poised to sit southeast of the\nforecast area. Post-frontal scattered to numerous rain showers\nwill encompass areas along and south of the I-64 corridor\nthrough Saturday morning. Chances drop considerably through the\nafternoon, with isolated to scattered showers and an isolated\nthunderstorms along the KY-TN and KY-VA borders. Shower chances\nshould generally stay south of a line extending from Monticello\nto Hazard to Prestonsburg.\n\nRain and thunderstorm chances will increase in coverage once\nagain overnight Saturday into Sunday morning. Deep moisture\n(PWATs around 1.25 to 1.50 inches) interacting with an\napproaching mid-level shortwave and low-level northeasterly flow\nwill generate a solid wave of overrunning rainfall, enhanced by\nupslope flow along the higher terrain. Rain chances will\ngradually decrease from northwest to southeast through Sunday\nafternoon as the trough axis pivots east and a reinforcing cold\nfront sweeps through the Ohio Valley. A wind shift to the\nnorthwest behind the front will usher in much drier air and cold\nair advection (CAA), ending precipitation by Sunday night.\n\nHeading into the new workweek, expansive surface high pressure\n(1024-1028 mb) builds directly into the Ohio Valley on Monday\nbefore drifting northeast toward New England and the Mid-Atlantic\nby Wednesday. Deep-layer subsidence under northwesterly flow\naloft will usher in a very dry air mass across the region,\nleading to clear skies and dry weather Monday through Wednesday.\n\nAs the high pressure shifts further off the East Coast on\nThursday, light southerly return flow will allow a brief\nmoderation in temperatures. However, a potent northern-stream\nshortwave will drive a strong reinforcing cold front through\nEastern Kentucky Thursday night, potentially bringing another\nsurge of cooler air heading into Friday.\n\nIn terms of sensible weather, daytime highs will generally range\nfrom the upper 60s to mid-70s through much of the period, before\nmoderating into the mid to upper 70s Wednesday and Thursday.\nOvernight lows will generally drop into the upper 40s to lower\n50s. The main exception will be Monday night and Tuesday night,\nwhen clear skies and light to calm winds create ideal\nradiational cooling conditions. Under this setup, ridge tops\nwill hold in the mid-40s while sheltered valleys dip into the\nupper 30s to lower 40s.",
